5.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {I continue to align with the Q synthesis because it best integrates the full body of published evidence without discarding a statement whose core clinical claim is directly corroborated by labeling, abuse-liability studies, and guideline rationale. II is unambiguously supported by SAMHSA/ACOG/product-label guidance that explicitly prefers the mono-product in pregnancy and documented naloxone-sensitivity cases to avoid precipitated withdrawal. III follows from consistent head-to-head PK/PD data showing negligible sublingual naloxone bioavailability (~0), so therapeutic safety and efficacy are equivalent and driven solely by the shared buprenorphine component when either formulation is taken as directed.  

V adds the route-of-administration distinction that explains why both products exist: naloxone’s deterrent effect is intentionally parenteral-specific while sublingual/oral therapeutic profiles remain comparable. Treating the single reversed word (“lack” for “presence”) as the obvious drafting slip it appears to be does not change that underlying evidence-based claim; the same literature that underpins II and III also documents precisely this differential misuse risk and prescribing rationale.  

R’s insistence on literal wording alone therefore under-weights the very data that drive real-world differential use, while Q captures the complete, guideline-consistent picture without introducing the inversions in I or the overstated uncertainty in IV. No new head-to-head studies have emerged to alter this balance, so II + III + V remains the combination that most accurately reflects how the evidence is applied in practice.}

